### Using conditions syntax with gsap.matchMedia()

Source: https://gsap.com/docs/v3/GSAP/gsap.matchMedia%28%29

Complete example of using the conditions syntax. The handler receives a context object with a conditions property containing booleans for each condition. It uses conditional logic to set rotation and duration based on isDesktop and reduceMotion. The handler can optionally return a cleanup function that will be called when none of the conditions match anymore; it will automatically call context.revert() - do not do that in the cleanup.

```javascript
let mm = gsap.matchMedia(),
  breakPoint = 800;

mm.add(
  {
    // set up any number of arbitrarily-named conditions. The function below will be called when ANY of them match.
    isDesktop: `(min-width: ${breakPoint}px)`,
    isMobile: `(max-width: ${breakPoint - 1}px)`,
    reduceMotion: "(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)",
  },
  (context) => {
    // context.conditions has a boolean property for each condition defined above indicating if it's matched or not.
    let { isDesktop, isMobile, reduceMotion } = context.conditions;

    gsap.to(".box", {
      rotation: isDesktop ? 360 : 180, // spin further if desktop
      duration: reduceMotion ? 0 : 2, // skip to the end if prefers-reduced-motion
    });

    return () => {
      // optionally return a cleanup function that will be called when none of the conditions match anymore (after having matched)
      // it'll automatically call context.revert() - do NOT do that here . Only put custom cleanup code here.
    };
  }
);
```

